$frontmatter {
  title: Schemas
  description: Eure Schema uses extensions to define types and constraints within Eure documents. The schema system is self-hosted, meaning the schema language is defined using itself.
}

"#": Schemas

"toc".$toc = true

@ "official-schemas" {
  "##": Official Schemas

  "body" = ```markdown
- `https://eure.dev/schemas/eure-schema/v1`
- `https://eure.dev/schemas/eure-template/v1`
- `https://eure.dev/schemas/eure-lint-config/v1`
- `https://eure.dev/schemas/eure-ls-config/v1`
- `https://eure.dev/schemas/eure-fmt-config/v1`
```

}

@ "overview" {
  "##": Overview

  "body" = ```markdown
Eure Schema is embedded within Eure documents using extension namespaces (prefixed with `$`). The meta-schema can be found in `assets/eure-schema.schema.eure`.
```

  @ "extension-levels" {
    "###": Extension Levels

    "body" = ```markdown
- `$foo` - Regular extensions used in documents
- `$ext-type.foo` - Extension type definitions in meta-schemas (defines the type of `$foo`)

---
```

  }

}

@ "core-concepts" {
  "##": Core Concepts

  @ "root-type" {
    "###": $root-type

    "body" = ```markdown
Specifies the root type of target documents validated by this schema.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
$schema = "my-config.schema.eure"

// This schema's root type is a record with specific fields
$root-type = {
  name = `text`
  version = `text`
}
```

  }

  @ "ext-type" {
    "###": $ext-type

    "body" = ```markdown
Defines extension types for specific paths in schemas. When users write documents, the `$ext-type` prefix is omitted.

**Schema side:**
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
field.$ext-type.optional = `boolean`
field.$ext-type.binding-style = `$types.binding-style`
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
**User side:**
```

    "eure-snippet-2" = ```eure
field.$optional = true
field.$binding-style = "section"
```

  }

  @ "types" {
    "###": $types

    "body" = ```markdown
Custom type definitions at document root. Types are referenced using `.$types.type-name`.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Define a custom type
@ $types.username
$variant: text
min-length = 3
max-length = 20
pattern = "^[a-z0-9_]+$"

// Use the custom type
@ user
name = `$types.username`
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
---
```

  }

}

@ "type-definitions" {
  "##": Type Definitions

  "body" = ```markdown
All types in Eure Schema are variants of a union type (`$types.type`).
```

  @ "primitive-types" {
    "###": Primitive Types

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// String type
name = `text`
name.$description: User's display name

// Integer type
age = `integer`
age.$optional = true

// Float type
score = `float`

// Boolean type
active = `boolean`

// Null type
deleted = `null`

// Any type (accepts any valid Eure value)
metadata = `any`
```

    "body" = ```markdown
**Shorthands:** `text`, `integer`, `float`, `boolean`, `null`, `any`

Shorthands are for simple types without constraints. For constraints, use the full form.
```

  }

  @ "text-type-with-constraints" {
    "###": Text Type with Constraints

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Full form (required for constraints)
@ username {
  $variant: text
  min-length = 3
  max-length = 20
  pattern = "^[a-z0-9_]+$"
}
```

  }

  @ "integer-type-with-constraints" {
    "###": Integer Type with Constraints

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ age {
  $variant: integer
  range = "[0, 150]"
}

// Rust-style range syntax
@ index {
  $variant: integer
  range = "0..100"      // 0 <= x < 100
}

// Or using shorthand with extensions
age = `integer`
age.$range = "0..=150"  // 0 <= x <= 150
```

  }

  @ "float-type-with-constraints" {
    "###": Float Type with Constraints

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ probability {
  $variant: float
  range = "[0.0, 1.0)"  // 0 <= x < 1
}

// Rust-style
temperature = `float`
temperature.$range = "-273.15.."  // x >= -273.15
```

  }

  @ "range-syntax" {
    "###": Range Syntax

    "body" = ```markdown
Two formats are supported:

**Rust-style** (left side always inclusive):
- `"0..100"` → 0 ≤ x < 100
- `"0..=100"` → 0 ≤ x ≤ 100
- `"0.."` → x ≥ 0
- `"..100"` → x < 100
- `"..=100"` → x ≤ 100

**Interval notation** (supports all 4 combinations):
- `"[0, 100]"` → 0 ≤ x ≤ 100 (both inclusive)
- `"[0, 100)"` → 0 ≤ x < 100 (left inclusive, right exclusive)
- `"(0, 100]"` → 0 < x ≤ 100 (left exclusive, right inclusive)
- `"(0, 100)"` → 0 < x < 100 (both exclusive)
- `"[0, )"` → x ≥ 0
- `"(, 100]"` → x ≤ 100
```

  }

  @ "text-type-with-language" {
    "###": Text Type with Language

    "body" = ```markdown
Text type supports optional language specifier for code blocks or semantic text.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Plain text
content = `text`

// Text with language
script = `text.javascript`
query = `text.sql`
email = `text.email`
url = `text.url`
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
**Shorthand:** `text`, `text.rust`, `text.email`, etc.

---
```

  }

}

@ "container-types" {
  "##": Container Types

  @ "record-type" {
    "###": Record Type

    "body" = ```markdown
Fixed named fields where each field has a specific name and type.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Shorthand (implicit record)
@ user
name = `text`
age = `integer`
email = `text.email`

// Explicit record variant
@ user {
  $variant: record
  name = `text`
  age = `integer`
}

// With unknown fields policy
@ config {
  $variant: record
  host = `text`
  port = `integer`
  $unknown-fields = "allow"  // or "deny" (default) or a type schema
}
```

  }

  @ "array-type" {
    "###": Array Type

    "body" = ```markdown
Ordered list of elements with the same type.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Shorthand
tags = [`text`]

// Full form
@ tags {
  $variant: array
  item = `text`
  min-length = 1
  max-length = 10
  unique = true
  contains = "required-tag"
}

// Nested arrays
matrix = [[`integer`]]
```

  }

  @ "map-type" {
    "###": Map Type

    "body" = ```markdown
Dynamic key-value pairs where all keys have the same type and all values have the same type.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Full form
@ headers {
  $variant: map
  key = `text`
  value = `text`
  min-size = 0
  max-size = 100
}
```

  }

  @ "tuple-type" {
    "###": Tuple Type

    "body" = ```markdown
Fixed-length, ordered elements where each position has a specific type.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Shorthand
point = (`float`, `float`)
rgb = (`integer`, `integer`, `integer`)

// Full form
@ coordinate {
  $variant: tuple
  elements = [`float`, `float`, `float`]
}
```

  }

  @ "variants" {
    "###": Union Type

    "body" = ```markdown
Tagged union that accepts one of multiple variant types.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Full form with named variants
@ $types.response {
  $variant: union
  variants.success = { data = `any` }
  variants.error = { message = `text`, code = `integer` }
}

// Using the union type
result = `$types.response`
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
**Note:** Union types always have a discriminator. Use `$interop.variant-repr` to customize representation.

---
```

  }

}

@ "variant-representation" {
  "##": Variant Representation

  "body" = ```markdown
Union types can be represented in different ways in the data model.
```

  @ "external-default" {
    "###": External (Default)

    "body" = ```markdown
The variant name wraps the content.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ $types.shape {
  $variant: union
  // Default: external representation
  variants.circle = { radius = `float` }
  variants.rectangle = { width = `float`, height = `float` }
}

// Data example:
// circle = { radius = 5.0 }
```

  }

  @ "internal" {
    "###": Internal

    "body" = ```markdown
Custom tag field name inside the content.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ $types.message {
  $variant: union
  $interop.variant-repr = { tag = "type" }
  variants.text = { content = `text` }
  variants.image = { url = `text` }
}

// Data example:
// { type = "text", content = "Hello" }
```

  }

  @ "adjacent" {
    "###": Adjacent

    "body" = ```markdown
Separate tag and content fields.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ $types.event {
  $variant: union
  $interop.variant-repr = { tag = "kind", content = "data" }
  variants.login = { username = `text` }
  variants.logout = { reason = `text` }
}

// Data example:
// { kind = "login", data = { username = "alice" } }
```

  }

  @ "untagged" {
    "###": Untagged

    "body" = ```markdown
No discriminator field (type is inferred from content).
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ $types.value {
  $variant: union
  $interop.variant-repr = "untagged"
  variants.string = `text`
  variants.number = `integer`
}

// Data example:
// "hello" or 42
```

  }

  @ "priority-ambiguity-resolution" {
    "###": Priority (Ambiguity Resolution)

    "body" = ```markdown
For untagged unions where multiple variants may match, use `priority` to specify resolution order.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ $types.response {
  $variant: union
  $interop.variant-repr = "untagged"
  priority: ["error", "success"]  // error takes precedence

  variants.error = { code = `integer`, message = `text` }
  variants.success = { data = `any` }
}

// If a value matches both error and success, error is selected
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
---
```

  }

}

@ "literal-type" {
  "##": Literal Type

  "body" = ```markdown
Literal type accepts only a specific constant value.
```

  "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// String literal
@ status {
  = "active"
  $variant: literal
}

// Using in unions for enum-like behavior
@ $types.log-level {
  $variant: union
  variants.debug = { = "debug", $variant: literal }
  variants.info = { = "info", $variant: literal }
  variants.warn = { = "warn", $variant: literal }
  variants.error = { = "error", $variant: literal }
}
```

  "body-2" = ```markdown
---
```

}

@ "type-references" {
  "##": Type References

  "body" = ```markdown
Reference other type definitions using path syntax.
```

  "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// Define types
$types.email = `text.email`
$types.user = {
  name = `text`
  email = `$types.email`
}

// Use type reference
contact = `$types.user`
```

  "body-2" = ```markdown
---
```

}

@ "cross-schema-type-references" {
  "##": Cross-Schema Type References

  "body" = ```markdown
Schemas can import and reference types from other schema files. Use this to keep shared primitives, common records, and organization-wide conventions in one place while each application schema stays focused on its own document shape.

Schema imports affect the schema graph only. A data document still points at one root schema with `$schema`; that root schema may then load other schema files through `$import`.
```

  @ "import" {
    "###": $import

    "body" = ```markdown
Declare imports at the root of a schema as a map from alias to schema path. The alias becomes the namespace used in type references.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// profile.schema.eure
$import = {
  common => "./common.schema.eure"
  auth => "./auth.schema.eure"
}

@ $types.profile {
  name = `$types.common.username`
  email = `$types.common.email`
  token = `$types.auth.jwt-token`
}

= `$types.profile`
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
Imported types are referenced as `` `$types.<alias>.<type-name>` ``.

| Reference form | Meaning |
|----------------|---------|
| `` `$types.user` `` | Local type declared in the same schema |
| `` `$types.common.user` `` | Type `user` exported by the schema imported as `common` |

Type references have exactly one optional import namespace. A schema can import another schema that imports more schemas, but callers cannot chain namespaces like `$types.common.base.user`. If you want to expose a transitive dependency, declare a local alias type in the imported schema and export that local type.
```

    "eure-snippet-2" = ```eure
// common.schema.eure
$import = {
  base => "./base.schema.eure"
}

// Re-export a dependency under common's own public API.
$types.username = `$types.base.username`
$export = ["username"]
```

    "body-3" = ```markdown
Only `$types` are imported. The imported schema's own root type is not automatically used by the importer; declare the reusable pieces under `$types` and reference those names explicitly.
```

  }

  @ "path-resolution" {
    "###": Import Path Resolution

    "body" = ```markdown
Local import paths are resolved relative to the schema file that contains the `$import` entry.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// schemas/app/profile.schema.eure
$import = {
  common => "../common/common.schema.eure"
  auth => "./auth.schema.eure"
}
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
Security rules keep imports predictable:

- Relative local paths are normalized before loading.
- Absolute local paths are rejected.
- Without a workspace config, imports cannot escape the root schema's directory.
- Inside a workspace, imports cannot escape the workspace root.
- `https://` imports are allowed only when the host is listed in `security.allowed-hosts` in `Eure.eure`.
- Other URL schemes are rejected.
- Import cycles are reported as schema conversion errors.
```

    "eure-snippet-2" = ```eure
// Eure.eure
security.allowed-hosts = ["schemas.example.com"]

@ targets.config
globs = ["config/**/*.eure"]
schema = "schemas/app.schema.eure"
```

    "eure-snippet-3" = ```eure
// schemas/app.schema.eure
$import = {
  shared => "https://schemas.example.com/shared.schema.eure"
}
```

    "body-3" = ```markdown
Remote imports use the same schema cache and cache-control flags as other remote schema loads. See [Project Configuration](/docs/project-configuration) and [CLI Reference](/docs/cli) for `--offline`, `--refresh`, and `--max-age`.
```

  }

  @ "export" {
    "###": $export

    "body" = ```markdown
`$export` defines the public type names that other schemas may reference. If `$export` is omitted, all locally declared `$types` are exported.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// common.schema.eure

@ $types.username {
  $variant: text
  pattern = `^[a-z0-9_]+$`
}

@ $types.email = `text.email`

@ $types.internal-id = `text`

// Only username and email are part of this schema's public API.
$export = ["username", "email"]
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
Schemas that import `common.schema.eure` can reference `$types.common.username` and `$types.common.email`, but `$types.common.internal-id` is rejected.

The export list must name types declared in the same schema's own `$types`. Imported names are not exported directly. To expose an imported type, define a local type reference and export the local name.
```

    "eure-snippet-2" = ```eure
// api.schema.eure
$import = {
  common => "./common.schema.eure"
}

$types.public-username = `$types.common.username`
$export = ["public-username"]
```

  }

  @ "complete-import-example" {
    "###": Complete Import Example

    "body" = ```markdown
**common.schema.eure** defines reusable public types:
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
$schema = "https://eure.dev/schemas/eure-schema/v1"

$export = ["username", "email"]

@ $types.username {
  $variant: text
  min-length = 3
  max-length = 20
  pattern = `^[a-z0-9_]+$`
}

@ $types.email = `text.email`
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
**profile.schema.eure** imports those public names and combines them with local fields:
```

    "eure-snippet-2" = ```eure
$schema = "https://eure.dev/schemas/eure-schema/v1"

$import = {
  common => "./common.schema.eure"
}

@ $types.profile {
  username = `$types.common.username`
  email = `$types.common.email`
  display-name = `text`
  display-name.$optional = true
}

= `$types.profile`
```

    "body-3" = ```markdown
**profile.eure** validates against the root schema only:
```

    "eure-snippet-3" = ```eure
$schema = "./profile.schema.eure"

username = `ada_lovelace`
email = email`ada@example.com`
display-name = "Ada Lovelace"
```

    "body-4" = ```markdown
Then run:
```

    "text-snippet" = ```text
$ eure check profile.eure
```

    "body-5" = ```markdown
`eure check` loads `profile.schema.eure`, follows its `$import` to `common.schema.eure`, verifies the export rules, and validates the data document through the resolved type references.
```

  }

  @ "errors-and-diagnostics" {
    "###": Errors and Diagnostics

    "body" = ```markdown
Import and export errors are reported while the schema is loaded:

| Situation | Result |
|-----------|--------|
| `$types.missing.user` with no `missing` import | Unknown import namespace |
| `$types.common.internal-id` when `internal-id` is not exported | Type is not exported by `common` |
| `$export = ["missing"]` | Exported name is not declared locally |
| `a.schema.eure` imports `b.schema.eure` and `b` imports `a` | Import cycle |
| Import path leaves the workspace boundary | Import resolver error |

When validation fails through an imported type, diagnostics point at both the data value and the schema definition that caused the failure, even when that definition lives in the imported schema file.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
// profile.schema.eure
$import = { common => "./common.schema.eure" }
name = `$types.common.username`
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
If `common.schema.eure` defines `username` with a pattern constraint, an invalid `name` value reports the data location in `profile.eure` and a secondary schema location in `common.schema.eure`.
```

  }

  @ "bundling" {
    "###": Distribution and Bundling

    "body" = ```markdown
The CLI and language tooling resolve schema imports directly when they load a schema. This applies to validation (`eure check`) and schema-aware format conversion (`eure to-json --schema ...`, `eure from-json --schema ...`).

A separate schema bundle command that writes a single self-contained schema file is not available yet. If you need to distribute schemas to an environment that cannot load local or remote imports, publish the imported schema files together and keep their relative paths unchanged.

---
```

  }

}

@ "metadata-extensions" {
  "##": Metadata Extensions

  @ "optional" {
    "###": $optional

    "body" = ```markdown
Marks a field as optional. Fields are required by default.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ user
name = `text`           // Required
bio = `text`
bio.$optional = true     // Optional
```

  }

  @ "description" {
    "###": $description

    "body" = ```markdown
Field description (supports plain text or rich markdown).
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ user
email = `text.email`
email.$description: User's primary email address for authentication.

// Or with markdown
email.$description = markdown`User's **primary** email address.`
```

  }

  @ "deprecated" {
    "###": $deprecated

    "body" = ```markdown
Marks a field as deprecated.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
old_field = `text`
old_field.$deprecated = true
```

  }

  @ "default" {
    "###": $default

    "body" = ```markdown
Default value for optional fields.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
timeout = `integer`
timeout.$optional = true
timeout.$default = 30
```

  }

  @ "examples" {
    "###": $examples

    "body" = ```markdown
Example values in Eure code format.
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
email = `text.email`
email.$examples = [eure`"user@example.com"`, eure`"admin@company.org"`]
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
---
```

  }

}

@ "binding-style" {
  "##": Binding Style

  "body" = ```markdown
Controls how document paths are represented.
```

  "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ field {
  $binding-style = "section"  // or "nested", "binding", "section-binding", etc.
}
```

  "body-2" = ```markdown
Options:
- `auto` - Automatically determine the best representation
- `passthrough` - Defer to subsequent keys
- `section` - Create a new section (`@ a.b.c`)
- `nested` - Create a nested section (`@ a.b.c { ... }`)
- `binding` - Bind value (`a.b.c = value`)
- `section-binding` - Section with block (`a.b.c { ... }`)
- `section-root-binding` - Section with root binding (`@ a.b.c = value`)

---
```

}

@ "unknown-fields-policy" {
  "##": Unknown Fields Policy

  "body" = ```markdown
Controls handling of fields not defined in the schema.
```

  "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ config {
  host = `text`
  port = `integer`

  // Reject unknown fields (default)
  $unknown-fields = "deny"

  // Or allow any unknown fields
  $unknown-fields = "allow"

  // Or validate unknown fields against a schema
  $unknown-fields = `text`
}
```

  "body-2" = ```markdown
---
```

}

@ "complete-example" {
  "##": Complete Example

  "eure-snippet" = ```eure
$schema = "eure-schema.schema.eure"

// Custom types
@ $types.username {
  $variant: text
  min-length = 3
  max-length = 20
  pattern = "^[a-z0-9_]+$"
}

@ $types.role {
  $variant: union
  variants.admin = { = "admin", $variant: literal }
  variants.user = { = "user", $variant: literal }
  variants.guest = { = "guest", $variant: literal }
}

// Main schema
@ $types.user {
  username = `$types.username`
  username.$description: Unique username for the account.

  email = `text.email`

  role = `$types.role`
  role.$default = "user"

  age = `integer`
  age.$optional = true
  age.$description: User's age in years.

  @ tags {
    $variant: array
    item = `text`
    unique = true
  }
  tags.$optional = true
}

// Set root type
$root-type = `$types.user`
```

  "body" = ```markdown
---
```

}

@ "design-decisions" {
  "##": Design Decisions

  @ "no-logical-operators" {
    "###": No Logical Operators

    "body" = ```markdown
Eure Schema does not adopt `allOf`/`anyOf`/`oneOf`/`not` from JSON Schema. Use union for alternatives and record for composition.
```

  }

  @ "no-format-attribute" {
    "###": No Format Attribute

    "body" = ```markdown
Instead of format strings, use typed text: `.text.email`, `.text.url`, `.text.uuid`, etc.
```

  }

  @ "nullable-types" {
    "###": Nullable Types

    "body" = ```markdown
Express nullable types using union with null:
```

    "eure-snippet" = ```eure
@ nullable-string {
  $variant: union
  variants.value = `text`
  variants.null = `null`
}
```

  }

  @ "discriminators" {
    "###": Discriminators

    "body" = ```markdown
Union types always have a discriminator. Customize with `$interop.variant-repr`:
- `"external"` - Default, variant name wraps content
- `"untagged"` - No discriminator
- `{ tag = "..." }` - Internal tagging
- `{ tag = "...", content = "..." }` - Adjacent tagging

---
```

  }

}

@ "type-checking-algorithm" {
  "##": Type Checking Algorithm

  "body" = ```markdown
Eure Schema uses a structural type checking algorithm that is both **sound** (accepted values always conform to the schema) and **complete** (all conforming values are accepted).
```

  @ "core-algorithm" {
    "###": Core Algorithm

    "body" = ```markdown
Type checking traverses the value structure recursively, matching each node against its corresponding schema:
```

    "snippet" = ```
check(value, schema) -> Result<(), TypeError>
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
| Value Type | Schema Type | Validation |
|------------|-------------|------------|
| Null | Null | Type match only |
| Bool | Boolean | const constraint |
| Integer | Integer | min/max, multiple_of, const, enum |
| Float | Float | min/max, const, enum |
| Text | Text | length, pattern, language, const, enum |
| Path | Path | path constraints |
| Hole | Any | Always passes (but marks document incomplete) |
| Array | Array | item type, min/max_items, unique, contains |
| Map | Map | key/value types, min/max_pairs |
| Tuple | Tuple | element types at each position |
| Map | Record | field types + unknown_fields_policy |
```

  }

  @ "union-type-checking-oneof-semantics" {
    "###": Union Type Checking (oneOf Semantics)

    "body" = ```markdown
Union types use **oneOf** semantics—exactly one variant must match:
```

    "snippet" = ```
check_union(value, union_schema):
  matching = []
  failures = []

  for (name, schema) in union_schema.variants:
    if check(value, schema).is_ok():
      matching.push(name)
    else:
      failures.push((name, error))

  match matching.len():
    0 -> return closest_error(failures)  // No match
    1 -> return Ok                        // Exactly one match
    _ -> resolve_ambiguity(matching, union_schema.priority)

resolve_ambiguity(matching, priority):
  if priority is set:
    for name in priority:
      if name in matching:
        return Ok  // First priority match wins
  return AmbiguityError(matching)
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
**Error Selection**: When no variant matches, the "closest" error is returned based on error depth (how deep into the structure the check progressed before failing).
```

  }

  @ "hole-values" {
    "###": Hole Values

    "body" = ```markdown
The hole value (`!`) represents an unfilled placeholder, similar to a "never" type:

- **Type checking**: Holes match any schema (always pass)
- **Completeness**: Documents containing holes are valid but not complete

Validation returns two flags:
```

    "rust-snippet" = ```rust
struct ValidationResult {
    is_valid: bool,    // No type errors (holes allowed)
    is_complete: bool, // No type errors AND no holes
    errors: Vec<TypeError>,
    warnings: Vec<Warning>,
}
```

  }

  @ "extension-validation" {
    "###": Extension Validation

    "body" = ```markdown
Extensions attached to nodes are validated against three sources:

1. **Schema-defined** (`$ext-type`): Extensions defined in the schema
2. **Built-in**: Extensions provided externally (e.g., `$schema`, `$variant`)
3. **Unknown**: Extensions not in either list

Unknown extensions pass validation but emit a **warning**.
```

    "snippet" = ```
check_extensions(node, schema):
  for (name, value) in node.extensions:
    if name in schema.ext_types:
      check(value, schema.ext_types[name])
    else if name in builtin_extensions:
      check(value, builtin_extensions[name])
    else:
      emit_warning(UnknownExtension(name))
```

    "body-2" = ```markdown
---
```

  }

}

@ "working-example" {
  "##": Working Example in Rust

  "body" = ```markdown
The following example shows the full flow: define a schema as an Eure document, convert it to a `SchemaDocument`, then validate data documents against it.

Add these dependencies to `Cargo.toml`:
```

  "toml-snippet" = ```toml
[dependencies]
eure-document = { path = "crates/eure-document" }
eure-schema = { path = "crates/eure-schema" }
```

  "body-2" = ```markdown
Then validate documents at runtime:
```

  "rust-snippet" = ```rust
use eure_document::eure;
use eure_schema::convert::document_to_schema;
use eure_schema::validate::validate;

// Step 1: Define a schema as an Eure document.
// Fields are required by default; use $optional = true to make them optional.
let schema_doc = eure!({
    name = `text`
    age = `integer`
    age.$optional = true
});

// Step 2: Convert the EureDocument into a SchemaDocument.
let (schema, _source_map) = document_to_schema(&schema_doc)
    .expect("schema conversion failed");

// Step 3: Validate a conforming document.
let valid_doc = eure!({
    name = "Alice"
    age = 30
});
let output = validate(&valid_doc, &schema);
assert!(output.is_valid);    // no type errors
assert!(output.is_complete); // no holes (!)

// Step 4: Validate a document that is missing the required `name` field.
let invalid_doc = eure!({ age = 30 });
let output = validate(&invalid_doc, &schema);
assert!(!output.is_valid);
for error in &output.errors {
    eprintln!("validation error: {error}");
}
```

  "body-3" = ```markdown
`ValidationOutput` fields:

| Field | Type | Meaning |
|-------|------|---------|
| `is_valid` | `bool` | No type errors (holes `!` are allowed) |
| `is_complete` | `bool` | No type errors **and** no holes |
| `errors` | `Vec<ValidationError>` | Type mismatches with path information |
| `warnings` | `Vec<ValidationWarning>` | Unknown extensions and other non-fatal issues |

---
```

}

@ "identical-schema" {
  "##": Identical Schema

  "body" = ```markdown
You can use another Eure file as a schema for another Eure file. This is convenient when you want to have various versions of the document, for example, for i18n or l10n.
```

}

@ "meta-schema" {
  "##": Meta-Schema

  "body" = ```markdown
The Eure Schema system is self-hosted. The complete meta-schema defining all schema constructs can be found in `assets/eure-schema.schema.eure`.
```

}
